ck coming back coming back  and when I came back to Paris I found  this Japanese Workshop Pottery Workshop  where I could start Pottery for the  first time  [Music]  and  once I touched clay it's almost as if  like sleeping thoughts awakened and  that's when you know things changed for  me  it's almost as if you feel  like there's sort of like electricity  coming from your hands throughout your  whole body and it awakens something that  seed inside and then  you  questioned your whole life again  I trained between Paris and Bizen in  Japan and Bizen is one of the six  ancestral  CD for pottery in Japan  there I was able to connect with  toshiaki shibuta who became a sort of  like Pottery father  him and his friends taught me the  kikuneri technique the spiral wedging  technique  it's sort of like Prince and the Clay's  memory the circular movements  and so it's easier for you to throw  he taught me so many things  um for example throwing slowly on the  wheel and taking your time while you  throw you don't have to throw fast  and let the clay absorb each of your  movements and you know  be slow with it be gentle with it  my friends in Japan taught me so many  things about their culture first of all  but also  you know about life because I lost my  dad very early on like when I was  eight nine so somehow through Ceramics I  sort of like found you know other  fathers and you know Big Brothers uh  that could teach me things  [Music]  we are very different  it's a different generation different  lifestyle  um different skin color  but we connect thanks to the clay and  for me that type of relationship is gold  at the beginning I was only making  functional pieces  and because I looked up to my Japanese  pottery fathers I wanted to be like them  but they also made me realize that no I  I cannot be like them and I should be me  that's when I started to make more  sculptural pieces  and realize that yes my energy wasn't a  polar energy it was more of an artist  energy someone that wants to create  different things and not make the same  thing over and over again  [Music]  when I was younger I used to be bullied  like  a lot and were sort of like not accepted  by by other peers and I was able to find  like security and peace in video games  and virtual world  where I could control the character  where I could choose the character I  want to be and and go on an adventure  games like Zelda or  [Music]  um Metal Gear Solid  it's so amazing to be able to pull these  Concepts and grow these Concepts that  you know you grew up with that you had  in your childhood and like drive them  with you into your adulthood and bring a  message out of it I  then was able to dig deeper in who I am  where I come from my roots  um from West Africa and Benin with the  spirituality and so on  when I train in Bizen I sense  spirituality and the way that my friends  were making their works  and that spirituality is highly infused  by the culture of Shinto and that made  me realize that that spirituality was  very similar to the one of voodoo in  Benin in West Africa and both religion  or a system of beliefs they share the  roots of animism and  there was this I don't know like the the  it resonated in me it also feeds you  know me as a maker  um in my studio when I create my work  because I use Clays from different  countries from both countries too  I don't have an academic background in  arts so I just use my intuition to put  colors together  but I have sort of like developed my own  theory about  the way I use color and one of them one  of the theories is that  for my work it's  80 percent  texture and 20 percent  pigment  um  and that's that's how I work so what  really brings life and gives life to the  color or the piece really is the texture  [Music]  working with the four elements Earth  fire air and water is amazing because  these elements are older than us human  beings and I truly believe that these  elements know more than we do it's  almost as if I'm asking questions about  my future and I'm Consulting the clay to  gain knowledge out of it  [Music]  I've had people asking me if it tears my  heart to you know apart from work or you  know just sell or work and see a piece  leave the studio and I'm like no like  the whole adventure of thinking of the  piece and then making it and having it  out there  um is the whole fun for me then what  comes at the end of that process is a  byproduct of the whole Adventure  foreign
 

